{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t02-002",
  "track": "track-02",
  "title": "ASP.NET Core startup host builder and application lifetime",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "The application entry point creates a builder, registers services, builds the application, configures endpoints and middleware, and runs the host. The builder phase configures infrastructure; the built app phase describes request handling.",
    "WebApplicationBuilder and WebApplication are the recommended minimal-host APIs used by current templates. CreateBuilder loads common configuration and logging defaults; CreateSlimBuilder is a smaller starting point whose omitted defaults may need to be added deliberately.",
    "The host composes server, dependency injection, configuration, logging, and lifetime management. Generic Host abstractions are shared with worker processes; older WebHost and Startup patterns remain important for existing applications but are compatibility-era patterns.",
    "The host starts hosted services and the web server, signals application lifetime events, and performs graceful shutdown within configured timeouts. A service's startup or shutdown failure affects host lifecycle and deployment readiness.",
    "Startup code should validate required configuration early, avoid blocking asynchronous work, and keep service registration separate from per-request work. Scoped request services must not be captured by singleton startup components."
  ],
